            <ArticleTitle>Detection of adamantane-sensitive influenza A(H3N2) viruses in Australia, 2017: a cause for hope?</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2807/1560-7917.ES.2017.22.47.17-00731</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>For over a decade virtually all A(H3N2) influenza viruses have been resistant to the adamantane class of antivirals. However, during the 2017 influenza season in Australia, 15/461 (3.3%) adamantane-sensitive A(H3N2) viruses encoding serine at residue 31 of the M2 protein were detected, more than the total number identified globally during the last 6 years. A return to wide circulation of adamantane-sensitive A(H3N2) viruses would revive the option of using these drugs for treatment and prophylaxis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
